 paired seeds, first-look optimization, relative error denominator, finite-population bounds, precision vs realized error vs confidence. Could this falsely label an estimate accurate? Could target-dependent schedules unfairly obscure a better protocol? State limits rather than demand a perfect mathematical optimizer.\n- Seven fixtures, actual insertion oracle and fullCOUNT truth, represented population, dense\/negative\/sparse\/islands\/moderate gaps\/uneven density\/wide. Does density\/row width\/cache\/index\/TDE change accuracy itself, cost within budget, or freshness? Is there a decisive missing condition that would change a recommendation?\n- Actual static cost comparisons: alternating\/rotating order, fullCOUNT warmup\/carryover, parallel settings, counter scope, cache state, fresh connection effects, budget and cleanup. Which causal claims are invalid and which conditional method comparisons remain useful?\n-100-column service protocol48arms: control\/exact\/3%\/10%, index absent\/present, two update mixes, repeated observations, actual statement interval vs trial, arrival conservation\/censoring, CPU pinning, observer overhead, recovery. Verify reduced precision can be tested against the observed3% budget failures without calling a short window an SLO pass.5% service is intentionally unmeasured.\n- Success\/completion\/error classification, per-script20min limit and aggregate guard, required outputs and artifact\/source hashes. Capture unexpected failures honestly, retain expected precision\/budget failures, do not treat process exit as accuracy or service approval. Does a failure prevent fixture cleanup or later arms?\n- Engineer objections and better alternatives: exactCOUNT on a small index may dominate sampling; metadata may be cheapest but has no universal10% guarantee; counters\/isolated replicas have freshness\/ownership\/maintenance costs. Avoid generalizing dense synthetic payloads or arbitrary unknown distributions.\nReport concrete counterexamples with source line\/function or result field, severity high\/middle\/low, stable IDs SOL-FRONTIER-*. No invented findings, style requests, blanket requests to remeasure all production, or \"few repeats\" without explaining a wrong decision. Only request local measurements that could change the recommended method.\nOutput adapter JSON {\"when\":{},\"payload\":{...}} with decision accepted\/rejected (design-readiness only), guidelineSha256 (snapshot guideline hash), reviewScope, findings, engineerQuestions, betterAlternatives, requestedExperiments, rolloutRecommendation, reviewEvidence, limitations, claimEvidenceMap, validationCoverageMatrix, priorityFixes. High\/middle open => rejected. Each finding has id,severity,section,scenario,evidence,impact,requiredCorrection. Include snapshot hash verification at start\/end and identify files actually read. Rollout remains pending actual experiments, white-database all and final two-pass Sol review even if design is accepted.","mergedVariables":{"_rielaInput":{"communicationIds":[],"messages":[],"sourceStepIds":[],"stepId":"sol-adversarial-review","workflowExecutionId":"temp-table-count-frontier-sol-session-8"},"availableMemories":{"node":[],"workflow":[]},"guidelineSha256":"29c9382c22d1f25f436176c112195280aa1d882383d1e8be234dc96c93995387","manifest":"results\/reference\/20261107-frontier-design\/manifest.json","memoryCommandHelp":"","nodeId":"sol-adversarial-review","nodeKind":"worker","purpose":"Design-readiness adversarial review of3\/5\/10% accuracy-cost comparisons; no DB operations. Immutable snapshot permits later author changes without invalidating this reviewed revision.","runtimeVariables":{"_rielaInput":{"communicationIds":[],"messages":[],"sourceStepIds":[],"stepId":"sol-adversarial-review","workflowExecutionId":"temp-table-count-frontier-sol-session-8"},"guidelineSha256":"29c9382c22d1f25f436176c112195280aa1d882383d1e8be234dc96c93995387","manifest":"results\/reference\/20261107-frontier-design\/manifest.json","purp